     ssyyssllooggcc — collect messages from syslog memory buffer

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssyyssllooggcc [--CCccffoo] [--nn _l_i_n_e_s] [--ss _r_e_p_o_r_t_i_n_g___s_o_c_k_e_t] _l_o_g_n_a_m_e
     ssyyssllooggcc --qq

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ssyyssllooggcc collects messages from the syslogd(8) memory buffer specified by
     the _l_o_g_n_a_m_e argument.

     For ssyyssllooggcc to work, syslogd(8) must be configured with one or more mem‐
     ory buffer logs (see syslog.conf(5) for details) and have a reporting
     socket location specified on the command line (using the --ss option to
     syslogd(8)).

     By default, ssyyssllooggcc will query the specified log and return all entries
     to standard output.

     The options are as follows:

     --CC      Request that the log buffer be cleared without reading it.

     --cc      Request that the log buffer be cleared once it has been read.

     --ff      Print out the last 10 lines and read from the buffer continu‐
             ously.  Like the --ff option in tail(1).

     --nn _l_i_n_e_s
             Print the specified number of lines from the end of the buffer.

     --oo      Check whether the specified log has overflowed.  If the log has
             overflowed, then a message will be printed to stdout(4) and the
             exit status will be set to 1.

     --qq      Request a list of available logs.  If a log has overflowed, an
             asterisk (‘*’) will be appended to its name.

     --ss _r_e_p_o_r_t_i_n_g___s_o_c_k_e_t
             Specify alternate reporting socket location (the default is
             _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_s_y_s_l_o_g_d_._s_o_c_k).

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     syslog(3), syslog.conf(5), syslogd(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ssyyssllooggcc command first appeared in OpenBSD 3.5.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
     The buffer space used for writing logs through the socket is limited.
     Thus it is possible to lose logs when running in continuous mode.  Losses
     are reported on standard error.

